<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-2-1">
  <title>Resources for 2/1</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of a <em>relation</em>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Understand relations as ways of describing relationships between pairs of objects.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use appropriate models (e.g. directed graph model) to understand relations.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given a description of a relation, list ordered pairs in the relation.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-intro-relations">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Introduction to Relations</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>relations</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />

    <problem>
      <title>Introduction to Relations</title>
      <p>
        For the following, recall that if <m>R</m> is a relation, the statement <m>aRb</m> means that <m>a</m> is related to <m>b</m> under the relation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Come up with some relations (human, number, set, and other) apart from the ones discussed in the video.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For the relations <m>\le</m> (i.e. less than or equal), <m>\mid</m> (i.e. divisibility), and <m>\subseteq</m> (i.e. subset relation), define the relation formally as a set of ordered pairs, using set builder notation.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find 4 elements of each relation in #2 (i.e. 4 ordered pairs).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Define the hostility relationship <m>H</m> on the set <m>P</m> of all people where <m>aHb</m> means that <m>a</m> is mad at <m>b</m>. Let Jojo be a person.  Describe the following set, interpreting <m>H</m> as a set of ordered pairs, and using set builder notation:  The set of people who are mad at Jojo (poor Jojo!).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Do what you did in #3 for all the other relations we discussed in class.  Describe the sets informally, and then describe them formally using set builder notation.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-relation-models">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Models of Relations</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>models we use to understand relations</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />

    <problem>
      <title>More Relations</title>
      <p>
        Work the following relation problems.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              For a relation <m>R \subseteq A \times B</m> and for <m>b \in B</m>, let <m>R[b]</m> be the set of elements of <m>A</m> that are related to <m>b</m>.  Write <m>R[b]</m> formally as a set in set builder notation.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              We define a relation <m>E</m> on <m>\mathbb Z</m>, where <m>aEb</m> means <m>a \equiv b</m> (mod 3).
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Define <m>E</m> as a subset of <m>{\mathbb Z} \times {\mathbb Z}</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    What numbers are in <m>E[1]</m>?  In <m>E[5]</m>?
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>n \in {\mathbb Z}</m>, and define <m>E</m> on <m>\mathbb Z</m>, where <m>aEb</m> means <m>a \equiv b</m> (mod <m>n</m>).
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Define <m>E</m> as a subset of <m>{\mathbb Z} \times {\mathbb Z}</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Let <m>b \in {\mathbb Z}</m>.  What are the elements in <m>E[b]</m>?
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove the relations in #2 and #3 are reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 7.1 (Sundstrom): pp. 362-370
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>